\title{Cantor's Hierarchy of Infinities:\\ A Self-Contained Development}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-13}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We assemble the classical picture of Cantor's transfinite hierarchy of cardinal
numbers and prove the landmark facts that separate one infinity from the next.
Starting from Cantor's theorem---every set is strictly smaller than its power
set---we establish that there is no largest cardinal, exhibit an explicit
strictly increasing tower of infinities obtained by iterated power sets, and
identify the bottom of the ladder: $\aleph_0$ as the least infinite cardinal and
$\aleph_1$ as the least uncountable one. We prove the existence of the Hartogs
number, giving a strictly larger well-orderable cardinal above any set. We treat
the size of the continuum $\cont = 2^{\aleph_0}$: it is uncountable, equinumerous
with both the power set of the naturals and the real line, and absorbs
multiplication ($\cont \cdot \cont = \cont$), so the plane has the same
cardinality as the line. By K\"onig's theorem the continuum has uncountable
cofinality. Finally we state the Continuum Hypothesis precisely, prove it equivalent to
the non-existence of an intermediate cardinal, and derive it from the Generalized
Continuum Hypothesis. All results are developed within standard axiomatic set
theory including the axiom of choice.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The discovery that infinity is not a single magnitude but an unbounded hierarchy
of strictly increasing magnitudes is among the most consequential ideas in modern
mathematics. Georg Cantor's insight was methodological before it was
substantive: to compare sizes of infinite collections one abandons counting in
favor of \emph{bijection}. Two sets have the same cardinality when their elements
can be placed in one-to-one correspondence. From this single move flows the
entire theory of transfinite cardinals.

This paper presents a self-contained development of the classical skeleton of
that theory. We treat, in order, Cantor's theorem and its corollaries; the
Hartogs construction; the least infinite and least uncountable cardinals; the
explicit Cantor (beth) tower; the arithmetic of the continuum; and the Continuum
Hypothesis together with its generalized form.

\subsection{Notation and conventions}

For a set $\alpha$ we write $\#\alpha$ for its cardinality. Cardinals are compared
by $\#\alpha \le \#\beta$, meaning there is an injection $\alpha \hookrightarrow
\beta$, and $\#\alpha < \#\beta$ when additionally no such injection exists in
reverse. We write $\Pow(\alpha)$ for the power set and identify subsets with
indicator functions, so $\#\Pow(\alpha) = 2^{\#\alpha}$. The least infinite
cardinal is $\aleph_0$; the aleph function $o \mapsto \aleph_o$ and beth function
$o \mapsto \beth_o$ are indexed by ordinals $o$. The continuum is $\cont =
2^{\aleph_0}$. We work in standard axiomatic set theory with the axiom of choice.

\section{Cantor's theorem and the absence of a largest cardinal}

\begin{theorem}[Cantor]\label{thm:cantor}
For every set $\alpha$, $\ \#\alpha < \#\Pow(\alpha)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The map $x \mapsto \{x\}$ injects $\alpha$ into $\Pow(\alpha)$, giving
$\#\alpha \le \#\Pow(\alpha)$. For strictness it suffices that no
$f : \alpha \to \Pow(\alpha)$ is surjective. Given such $f$, set
$D = \{ x \in \alpha : x \notin f(x)\}$. If $D = f(a)$, then
$a \in D \iff a \notin f(a) = D$, a contradiction. Hence $D$ is not in the image.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
For every set $\alpha$ there is no surjection $f : \alpha \to \Pow(\alpha)$ and no
injection $g : \Pow(\alpha) \to \alpha$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Non-surjectivity is the diagonal argument. An injection $\Pow(\alpha) \to \alpha$
would yield, via a left inverse, a surjection $\alpha \to \Pow(\alpha)$,
contradicting the first part.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No largest cardinal]
For every cardinal $c$ there is a cardinal $d$ with $c < d$; explicitly $d = 2^c$,
since $c < 2^c$.
\end{corollary}

Iterating this corollary produces an endless ascending sequence of cardinals: the
hierarchy of infinities has no maximum.

\section{The Hartogs number}

\begin{theorem}[Hartogs]
For every set $\alpha$ there is an ordinal $o$ with $\#\alpha < \#o$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $o$ to be the ordinal underlying the successor cardinal $(\#\alpha)^{+}$. The
cardinality of the ordinal underlying a cardinal is that cardinal, so
$\#o = (\#\alpha)^{+} > \#\alpha$.
\end{proof}

The Hartogs number guarantees a strictly larger \emph{well-ordered} size,
independently of whether the power set can be well-ordered; it drives the aleph
hierarchy upward via the successor cardinal.

\section{The bottom of the ladder: $\aleph_0$ and $\aleph_1$}

\begin{theorem}[$\aleph_0$ least infinite]
For every infinite set $\alpha$, $\ \aleph_0 \le \#\alpha$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An infinite set admits an injection from $\N$ (choose distinct elements
recursively), so its cardinality is at least $\aleph_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$\aleph_1$ is the first uncountable cardinal, defined as $\aleph_0^{+}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
$\aleph_1 = \aleph_0^{+}$, and consequently $\aleph_0 < \aleph_1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By definition of the aleph function, $\aleph_1 = (\aleph_0)^{+}$, and
$\aleph_0 < \aleph_0^{+}$ is the defining property of a successor.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\aleph_1$ least uncountable]
If $c$ is a cardinal with $\aleph_0 < c$, then $\aleph_1 \le c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $\aleph_1 = \aleph_0^{+}$ and $c^{+} \le d \iff c < d$, we get
$\aleph_1 = \aleph_0^{+} \le c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity of the alephs]
If $o_1 < o_2$ then $\aleph_{o_1} < \aleph_{o_2}$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The Cantor (beth) tower}

\begin{definition}[Cantor tower]
Define $T : \N \to \Card$ by $T_0 = \aleph_0$ and $T_{n+1} = 2^{T_n}$. These are
the finite beth numbers, $T_n = \beth_n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
For every $n$, $T_n < T_{n+1}$; hence $T$ is strictly increasing.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$T_n < 2^{T_n} = T_{n+1}$ is Cantor's theorem applied to $T_n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
Every rung satisfies $\aleph_0 \le T_n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction: $T_0 = \aleph_0$; if $\aleph_0 \le T_n$ then
$\aleph_0 \le T_n < T_{n+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
$T_1 = 2^{\aleph_0} = \cont$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Aleph below beth]
For every ordinal $o$, $\ \aleph_o \le \beth_o$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both hierarchies agree at $0$ and at limits; at successors
$\aleph_{o+1} = \aleph_o^{+} \le 2^{\aleph_o}$ while
$\beth_{o+1} = 2^{\beth_o}$. Transfinite induction propagates the inequality.
\end{proof}

\section{The size of the continuum}

\begin{theorem}[Uncountability of the reals]
$\aleph_0 < \#\R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\#\R = 2^{\aleph_0}$ and $\aleph_0 < 2^{\aleph_0}$ by Cantor's theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reals as subsets of $\N$]
$\#\Pow(\N) = \#\R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\#\Pow(\N) = 2^{\aleph_0} = \cont = \#\R$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Absorption]
$\cont \cdot \cont = \cont$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For any infinite $\kappa$, $\kappa \cdot \kappa = \kappa$; apply with
$\kappa = \cont$ since $\aleph_0 \le \cont$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Plane equals line]
$\#(\R \times \R) = \#\R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\#(\R \times \R) = \cont \cdot \cont = \cont = \#\R$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uncountable cofinality of $\cont$]
$\aleph_0 < \cf(\cont)$; consequently $\cont$ is never the supremum of a countable
family of strictly smaller cardinals.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
K\"onig's theorem gives $\kappa < \cf(2^{\kappa})$ for infinite $\kappa$; take
$\kappa = \aleph_0$ and use $2^{\aleph_0} = \cont$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[K\"onig, general form]
For every infinite cardinal $\kappa$, $\ \kappa < \cf(2^{\kappa})$; equivalently
$2^{\kappa}$ is never a $\kappa$-indexed supremum of smaller cardinals.
\end{theorem}

This excludes, for instance, $2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_\omega$, since
$\aleph_\omega$ has countable cofinality.

\section{The Continuum Hypothesis}

\begin{definition}[Continuum Hypothesis]
The Continuum Hypothesis (CH) is the statement $\cont = \aleph_1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[CH as no intermediate cardinal]
$\mathrm{CH} \iff \neg\, \exists\, c \ \text{with}\ \aleph_0 < c < \cont$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) If $\cont = \aleph_0^{+}$, then $c < \cont$ forces
$c \le \aleph_0$, so no $c$ lies strictly between. ($\Leftarrow$) Always
$\aleph_1 \le \cont$ by uncountability of $\cont$ and minimality of $\aleph_1$;
if $\aleph_1 < \cont$ then $\aleph_1$ is itself intermediate, contradiction, so
$\cont = \aleph_1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Generalized Continuum Hypothesis]
GCH is the statement that for every ordinal $o$, $\ 2^{\aleph_o} = \aleph_{o+1}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[GCH $\Rightarrow$ CH]
The Generalized Continuum Hypothesis implies the Continuum Hypothesis.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Specialize to $o = 0$: $\cont = 2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[GCH collapses beth to aleph]
Under GCH, for every ordinal $o$, $\ \beth_o = \aleph_o$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Transfinite induction: $\beth_0 = \aleph_0$; successor
$\beth_{o+1} = 2^{\beth_o} = 2^{\aleph_o} = \aleph_{o+1}$; limits are suprema that
agree termwise.
\end{proof}

CH was the first of Hilbert's problems. Its ultimate resolution is
metamathematical: CH is independent of the standard axioms of set theory
(G\"odel established consistency of CH, Cohen consistency of its negation). The
theorems above are exactly those that survive this independence.

\section{Cardinal-arithmetic laws}

\begin{theorem}
For every infinite cardinal $\kappa$:
$\ \kappa + \kappa = \kappa$, $\ \kappa \cdot \kappa = \kappa$, and
$\ \kappa^{\kappa} = 2^{\kappa}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first two are the classical absorption laws. For the third,
$2^{\kappa} \le \kappa^{\kappa} \le (2^{\kappa})^{\kappa} = 2^{\kappa\cdot\kappa}
= 2^{\kappa}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion and future work}

The development isolates the robust core of transfinite cardinal theory: the
facts holding in every model of the standard axioms. A recurring theme is the
tension between the power-set jump $c \mapsto 2^c$ and the successor jump
$c \mapsto c^{+}$; the inequality $\aleph_o \le \beth_o$ measures their
divergence, and GCH is exactly the hypothesis that they never diverge.

Future work includes: a precise treatment of beth fixed points $\kappa = \beth_\kappa$ via
the fixed-point theorem for normal functions; deriving concrete K\"onig
exclusions of candidate values of $\cont$; stating the metamathematical
independence of CH; and giving a choice-free reformulation of the Hartogs
construction.

